## One-line Interview Answer

**Inheritance in Java is an OOP mechanism where a child class inherits the properties and behaviors of a parent class using the `extends` keyword, enabling code reuse and hierarchical relationships.**

## Method Overloading vs Method Overriding (Java)

Both provide **polymorphism**, but they work differently.

---

## 1. Method Overloading

**Method Overloading = same method name with different parameters in the same class.**

Rules:

```text
method name → same
parameters → different
class → same
```

Example:

```java
class Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Calculator c = new Calculator();

        System.out.println(c.add(5,10));
        System.out.println(c.add(5,10,15));
        System.out.println(c.add(5.5,2.5));

    }

}
```

Output

```
15
30
8.0
```

Resolved at:

```
Compile Time
```

So overloading = **compile-time polymorphism**.

---

## 2. Method Overriding

**Method Overriding = child class provides new implementation of parent method.**

Rules:

```text
method name → same
parameters → same
class → parent + child
```

Example:

```java
class Animal {

    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes sound");
    }

}
```

Child class:

```java
class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Animal a = new Dog();

        a.sound();

    }

}
```

Output

```
Dog barks
```

Resolved at:

```
Runtime
```

So overriding = **runtime polymorphism**.

---

## Key Differences

| Feature     | Overloading  | Overriding               |
| ----------- | ------------ | ------------------------ |
| Method name | Same         | Same                     |
| Parameters  | Different    | Same                     |
| Class       | Same class   | Parent + Child           |
| Return type | Can differ   | Must be same / covariant |
| Binding     | Compile time | Runtime                  |
| Annotation  | Not required | `@Override` recommended  |

---

## Visual Example

```
Overloading

add(int,int)
add(int,int,int)
add(double,double)
```

```
Overriding

Animal → sound()
Dog    → sound()
```

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**Method overloading allows multiple methods with the same name but different parameters in the same class, while method overriding allows a child class to provide a new implementation of a parent class method with the same signature.**

## Dynamic Polymorphism in Java

**Dynamic Polymorphism = method call resolved at runtime based on the actual object type.**

It is achieved using **method overriding**.

Also called:

```text
Runtime Polymorphism
```

---

## Basic Example

Parent class:

```java
class Animal {

    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes sound");
    }

}
```

Child class:

```java
class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }

}
```

Another child:

```java
class Cat extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Cat meows");
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Animal a1 = new Dog();
        Animal a2 = new Cat();

        a1.sound();
        a2.sound();

    }

}
```

Output

```text
Dog barks
Cat meows
```

---

## Why It Is Called Dynamic

Because the JVM decides **which method to execute at runtime**.

```text
Animal a = new Dog();

compile time → reference type = Animal
runtime      → object type = Dog
```

So JVM calls:

```text
Dog.sound()
```

