```text
Drawing circle
```

JVM selects **Circle.draw()** at runtime.

---

## Key Requirements

Dynamic polymorphism requires:

```text
Inheritance
Method overriding
Parent reference → child object
```

Example pattern:

```java
Animal a = new Dog();
```

---

## Compile-Time vs Runtime

| Type                      | Example            |
| ------------------------- | ------------------ |
| Compile-time polymorphism | Method overloading |
| Runtime polymorphism      | Method overriding  |

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**Dynamic polymorphism in Java is the ability of a parent reference to call different implementations of a method depending on the actual object type at runtime, typically achieved through method overriding.**

## Multiple Inheritance Problem in Java

**Multiple inheritance problem (Diamond Problem)** occurs when a class inherits from **two parent classes that contain the same method**, causing ambiguity about which method should be used.

Java **does not allow multiple inheritance with classes** to avoid this problem.

---

## Diamond Problem Structure

```
       A
      / \
     B   C
      \ /
       D
```

Class `D` inherits from both `B` and `C`, which both inherit from `A`.

If both `B` and `C` override a method from `A`, Java cannot decide **which version to use**.

---

## Example (Conceptual Problem)

```java
class A {

    void show() {
        System.out.println("Class A");
    }

}

class B extends A {

    void show() {
        System.out.println("Class B");
    }

}

class C extends A {

    void show() {
        System.out.println("Class C");
    }

}
```

If Java allowed:

```java
class D extends B, C { }
```

Then calling:

```java
D d = new D();
d.show();
```

Ambiguity:

```
Should JVM call B.show() ?
or
C.show() ?
```

This is the **diamond problem**.

---

## Why Java Avoids It

Java prevents ambiguity by **not allowing multiple inheritance with classes**.

This code is illegal:

```java
class D extends B, C { }  // Compile-time error
```

---

## How Java Solves It

Java allows **multiple inheritance using interfaces**.

Example:

```java
interface A {

    void show();

}

interface B {

    void display();

}
```

Implementation:

```java
class C implements A, B {

    public void show() {
        System.out.println("Show method");
    }

    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Display method");
    }

}
```

---

## Java 8 Default Method Conflict

If two interfaces have the same default method:

```java
interface A {

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("A");
    }

}

interface B {

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("B");
    }

}
```

Child class must resolve it:

```java
class C implements A, B {

    public void show() {
        A.super.show();
    }

}
```

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**The multiple inheritance problem (diamond problem) occurs when a class inherits from two classes that contain the same method, causing ambiguity about which method should be used; Java avoids this by not supporting multiple inheritance with classes.**

## How Interfaces Solve the Diamond Problem in Java

The **diamond problem** occurs when a class inherits the same method from **two parent types**, causing ambiguity.

Java solves this using **interfaces** and **explicit method implementation rules**.

---

## Diamond Structure

```
        A
       / \
      B   C
       \ /
        D
```

If both `B` and `C` contain the same method, class `D` must decide **which implementation to use**.

---

# Case 1 — Interfaces Without Default Methods

Interfaces only declare methods (before Java 8).

Example:

```java
interface A {
    void show();
}

interface B {
    void show();
}
```

Implementation:

```java
class C implements A, B {

    public void show() {
        System.out.println("Implementation in C");
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        C obj = new C();
        obj.show();

    }

}
```

Output

```
Implementation in C
```

No ambiguity because **class must implement the method**.

---

# Case 2 — Java 8 Default Method Conflict

Java 8 introduced **default methods**.

Example:

```java
interface A {

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("A show");
    }

}

interface B {

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("B show");
    }

}
```

Implementation must resolve conflict:

```java
class C implements A, B {

    public void show() {
        A.super.show();
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        C obj = new C();
        obj.show();

    }

}
```

Output

```
A show
```

---

# Why Interfaces Solve Diamond Problem

Java forces **explicit resolution**.

Rules:

```
1. Class method > Interface default method
2. Child class must override conflicting default methods
3. Specific interface can be called using Interface.super.method()
```

---

# Example Showing Rule 1

```java
class A {

    void show() {
        System.out.println("Class A");
    }

}

interface B {

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("Interface B");
    }

}

class C extends A implements B { }
```

Usage:

```java
C obj = new C();
obj.show();
```

Output

```
Class A
```

Because:

```
Class method > Interface default method
```

---

# One-line Interview Answer

**Interfaces solve the diamond problem by forcing the implementing class to explicitly override conflicting methods, eliminating ambiguity between multiple parent implementations.**

## Abstract Class vs Interface (Java)

Both are used to achieve **abstraction**, but they differ in **design, capabilities, and usage**.

---

## Abstract Class

An **abstract class** can contain **both abstract and concrete methods**.

Example:

```java
abstract class Animal {

    abstract void sound();

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }

}
```

Child class:

```java
class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Animal a = new Dog();

        a.sound();
        a.eat();

    }

}
```

---

## Interface

An **interface defines a contract** that classes must implement.

Example:

```java
interface Payment {

    void pay(double amount);

}
```

Implementation:

```java
class UpiPayment implements Payment {

    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("UPI Payment: " + amount);
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
Payment p = new UpiPayment();
p.pay(500);
```

---

## Key Differences

| Feature              | Abstract Class             | Interface                                    |
| -------------------- | -------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Methods              | Abstract + concrete        | Abstract (default/static allowed in Java 8+) |
| Variables            | Instance variables allowed | Only `public static final`                   |
| Constructors         | Allowed                    | Not allowed                                  |
| Inheritance          | `extends`                  | `implements`                                 |
| Multiple inheritance | Not supported              | Supported                                    |
| Access modifiers     | Any                        | Methods public by default                    |

---

## Example Showing Multiple Interfaces

```java
interface A {
    void show();
}

interface B {
    void display();
}

class C implements A, B {

    public void show() {
        System.out.println("show");
    }

    public void display() {
        System.out.println("display");
    }

}
```

---

## When to Use

Use **Abstract Class** when:

```text
Classes share common behavior
Need constructors
Need instance variables
```

Use **Interface** when:

```text
Define capability/contract
Multiple inheritance required
Loose coupling needed
```

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**An abstract class can contain both abstract and concrete methods with instance variables, while an interface defines a contract with methods that implementing classes must provide, supporting multiple inheritance.**

## SOLID Principles (Java)

**SOLID = 5 design principles used to build maintainable, scalable, and flexible object-oriented systems.**

```
S → Single Responsibility Principle
O → Open Closed Principle
L → Liskov Substitution Principle
I → Interface Segregation Principle
D → Dependency Inversion Principle
```

---

# 1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

**A class should have only one reason to change.**

Bad design:

```java
class Report {

    void generateReport() {
        System.out.println("Generating report");
    }

    void saveToFile() {
        System.out.println("Saving report");
    }

}
```

Better design:

```java
class ReportGenerator {

    void generate() {
        System.out.println("Generating report");
    }

}

class ReportSaver {

    void save() {
        System.out.println("Saving report");
    }

}
```

---

# 2. Open Closed Principle (OCP)

**Software should be open for extension but closed for modification.**

Example:

```java
interface Payment {

    void pay(double amount);

}
```

Implementations:

```java
class CreditCardPayment implements Payment {

    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("Credit Card Payment: " + amount);
    }

}

class UpiPayment implements Payment {

    public void pay(double amount) {
        System.out.println("UPI Payment: " + amount);
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
Payment payment = new UpiPayment();
payment.pay(500);
```

New payment method → **add new class without modifying existing code**.

---

# 3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

**Child classes must be replaceable with parent classes without breaking behavior.**

Example:

```java
class Bird {

    void fly() { }

}
```

Bad design:

```java
class Ostrich extends Bird {

    void fly() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException();
    }

}
```

Better design:

```java
interface Bird { }

interface FlyingBird extends Bird {
    void fly();
}
```

---

# 4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

**Clients should not be forced to implement methods they do not use.**

Bad design:

```java
interface Worker {

    void work();
    void eat();

}
```

Better design:

```java
interface Workable {
    void work();
}

interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}
```

---

# 5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

**High-level modules should depend on abstractions, not concrete implementations.**

Bad design:

```java
class MySQLDatabase {

    void connect() { }

}

class UserService {

    private MySQLDatabase db = new MySQLDatabase();

}
```

Better design:

```java
interface Database {
    void connect();
}
```

Implementation:

```java
class MySQLDatabase implements Database {

    public void connect() {
        System.out.println("MySQL connected");
    }

}
```

Service:

```java
class UserService {

    private Database db;

    UserService(Database db) {
        this.db = db;
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
Database db = new MySQLDatabase();
UserService service = new UserService(db);
```

---

## Quick Summary

| Principle | Meaning                                  |
| --------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| SRP       | One responsibility per class             |
| OCP       | Extend behavior without modifying code   |
| LSP       | Child objects must replace parent safely |
| ISP       | Smaller, specific interfaces             |
| DIP       | Depend on abstractions                   |

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**SOLID principles are five object-oriented design principles (SRP, OCP, LSP, ISP, DIP) that help developers build maintainable, flexible, and scalable software systems.**

Java **TreeMap** me **hash collision ka concept hota hi nahi**. Collision ka concept **HashMap** me hota hai.

**Reason:**
`TreeMap` internally **Red-Black Tree** use karta hai, hashing nahi.

---

**TreeMap working (simplified):**

```java
TreeMap<K,V> map = new TreeMap<>();

map.put(10, "A");
map.put(20, "B");
map.put(5, "C");
```

Internally structure:

```
        10
       /  \
      5    20
```

Ordering based on:

```java
1. Comparable (natural ordering)
or
2. Comparator (custom ordering)
```

---

**Key comparison logic**

```java
if (comparator != null)
    comparator.compare(k1, k2);
else
    ((Comparable)k1).compareTo(k2);
```

---

**Duplicate key handling**

```java
TreeMap<Integer, String> map = new TreeMap<>();

map.put(10, "A");
map.put(10, "B");

System.out.println(map);
```

Output

```
{10=B}
```

Because internally:

```java
if(compare(key, existingKey) == 0){
    replaceValue();
}
```

---

**Internal Node structure (simplified)**

```java
static final class Entry<K,V> {
    K key;
    V value;
    Entry<K,V> left;
    Entry<K,V> right;
    Entry<K,V> parent;
    boolean color; // RED or BLACK
}
```

---

**Contrast with HashMap collision**

HashMap me collision resolution:

```
Bucket -> LinkedList -> Tree (Java 8+)
```

Pseudo logic:

```java
if(bucketEmpty)
    insertNode();
else if(hashCollision)
    addToLinkedList();

if(listSize > 8)
    convertToRedBlackTree();
```

