## Functional Interfaces (Java)

A **functional interface** is an interface that contains **exactly one abstract method**.

It is mainly used with **Lambda expressions and Stream API**.

Annotation used:

```java
@FunctionalInterface
```

---

## Basic Example

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b);

}
```

Implementation using lambda:

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Calculator c = (a,b) -> a + b;

        System.out.println(c.add(5,10));

    }

}
```

Output

```
15
```

---

## Without Lambda (Traditional Way)

```java
class AddOperation implements Calculator {

    public int add(int a,int b){
        return a + b;
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
Calculator c = new AddOperation();
System.out.println(c.add(5,10));
```

Lambda simplifies this.

---

## Rules of Functional Interface

```
Must contain exactly 1 abstract method
Can contain multiple default methods
Can contain static methods
Can extend other interfaces
```

Example:

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface Printer {

    void print(String msg);

    default void show(){
        System.out.println("default method");
    }

    static void info(){
        System.out.println("static method");
    }

}
```

---

## Built-in Functional Interfaces

### Predicate

```java
Predicate<Integer> p = x -> x > 10;

System.out.println(p.test(15));
```

---

### Function

```java
Function<Integer,Integer> f = x -> x * 2;

System.out.println(f.apply(5));
```

---

### Consumer

```java
Consumer<String> c = s -> System.out.println(s);

c.accept("Java");
```

---

### Supplier

```java
Supplier<Double> s = () -> Math.random();

System.out.println(s.get());
```

---

## Example with Stream API

```java
List<Integer> list = List.of(1,2,3,4,5);

list.stream()
    .filter(x -> x % 2 == 0)
    .forEach(System.out::println);
```

Here:

```
filter → Predicate
forEach → Consumer
```

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**A functional interface is an interface that contains exactly one abstract method and is used as the target type for lambda expressions and method references in Java.**

Yes. A **Functional Interface** **can extend another interface**, but there is **one strict rule**.

**Restriction:**
After inheritance, the interface must still have **exactly one abstract method**.

---

**Case 1 — Valid (still one abstract method)**

```java
interface A {
    void run();
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface B extends A {
}
```

Usage

```java
B obj = () -> System.out.println("Hello");
obj.run();
```

✔ Valid because total abstract methods = **1**

---

**Case 2 — Invalid (two abstract methods)**

```java
interface A {
    void run();
}

interface C {
    void stop();
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface B extends A, C { }
```

❌ Compile error

Reason

```text
Multiple abstract methods found
```

---

**Case 3 — Valid (default methods allowed)**

```java
interface A {
    void run();
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface B extends A {

    default void log(){
        System.out.println("log");
    }
}
```

✔ Allowed because **default methods don't count**

---

**Case 4 — Valid (methods from Object allowed)**

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface Test {

    boolean equals(Object obj);

    void execute();
}
```

✔ Allowed because methods from **Object class** don't count.

---

**Interview One-liner**

```text
A functional interface can extend other interfaces, but after inheritance it must still contain exactly one abstract method.
```

---

**Real Example from Java**

```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface UnaryOperator<T>
        extends java.util.function.Function<T,T> {
}
```

Here **UnaryOperator** extends **Function**, yet remains functional.

