## Lambda Expression (Java)

A **Lambda expression** is a **short way to implement a functional interface using an anonymous function**.

Introduced in **Java 8**.

Syntax:

```java
(parameters) -> expression
```

or

```java
(parameters) -> { statements }
```

---

## Example Without Lambda

```java
interface Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b);

}
```

Implementation:

```java
class AddImpl implements Calculator {

    public int add(int a,int b){
        return a + b;
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
Calculator c = new AddImpl();
System.out.println(c.add(5,10));
```

---

## Same Example Using Lambda

```java
Calculator c = (a,b) -> a + b;

System.out.println(c.add(5,10));
```

Output

```
15
```

Lambda removes **boilerplate code**.

---

## Example with Runnable

Without Lambda:

```java
Runnable r = new Runnable() {

    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Thread running");
    }

};
```

With Lambda:

```java
Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("Thread running");
```

---

## Example with Collections

```java
List<Integer> list = List.of(1,2,3,4,5);

list.forEach(x -> System.out.println(x));
```

---

## Lambda with Multiple Statements

```java
Calculator c = (a,b) -> {

    int result = a + b;
    return result;

};
```

---

## Lambda + Streams Example

```java
List<Integer> list = List.of(1,2,3,4,5);

list.stream()
    .filter(x -> x % 2 == 0)
    .map(x -> x * 2)
    .forEach(System.out::println);
