**Design Patterns** are **reusable solutions to commonly occurring problems in software design**. They are **proven templates or best practices** that developers use to structure code in a scalable, maintainable, and reusable way.

They are **not finished code**, but rather **guidelines on how to organize classes and objects** to solve a recurring design problem.

Example: Instead of creating objects directly using `new`, we may use a **Factory Pattern** to centralize object creation.

```java
interface Notification {
    void send();
}

class EmailNotification implements Notification {
    public void send() {
        System.out.println("Sending Email");
    }
}

class SMSNotification implements Notification {
    public void send() {
        System.out.println("Sending SMS");
    }
}

class NotificationFactory {

    public static Notification create(String type) {

        if(type.equals("email"))
            return new EmailNotification();

        if(type.equals("sms"))
            return new SMSNotification();

        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid type");
    }
}
```

Usage

```java
Notification notification = NotificationFactory.create("email");
notification.send();
```

