Next?
👉 **Can functional interface extend another interface? (very important trap 🔥)**

Yes ✅ — a **functional interface can extend another interface**, but condition apply 🔥

---

## ✅ Case 1: Still Functional (VALID)

👉 Total abstract methods = **1**

```java id="n7k2p1"
interface A {
    void a();
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface B extends A {
    // no new abstract method
}
```

✔️ Still functional (only 1 abstract method)

---

## ✅ Case 2: Override Same Method (VALID)

```java id="r3x8v6"
interface A {
    void a();
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface B extends A {
    void a(); // same method (override)
}
```

✔️ Still counts as **one abstract method**

---

## ❌ Case 3: Multiple Abstract Methods (INVALID)

```java id="t9m4q2"
interface A {
    void a();
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface B extends A {
    void b(); // ❌ now total = 2 abstract methods
}
```

❌ Not a functional interface

---

## ✅ Case 4: Default Methods (VALID)

```java id="k5p1z7"
interface A {
    void a();
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface B extends A {
    default void log() {
        System.out.println("log");
    }
}
```

✔️ Still valid

---

## Rule Summary 🔥

```java id="f2h8u0"
// VALID if total abstract methods = 1
// INVALID if total abstract methods > 1
```

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Yes, a functional interface can extend another interface **as long as the total number of abstract methods remains exactly one**.

