## Anonymous Class vs Lambda (Java 8)

---

### 1. Syntax

```java id="a1x1"
// Anonymous Class
Runnable r1 = new Runnable() {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Run");
    }
};
```

```java id="b2x2"
// Lambda
Runnable r2 = () -> System.out.println("Run");
```

---

### 2. Code Size

```java id="c3x3"
// Anonymous → verbose ❌
// Lambda → concise ✅
```

---

### 3. Compilation

```java id="d4x4"
// Anonymous → creates separate .class file

// Lambda → uses invokedynamic (no extra class file)
```

---

### 4. this Keyword 🔥

```java id="e5x5"
class Test {

    void demo() {

        Runnable r1 = new Runnable() {
            public void run() {
                System.out.println(this); // refers to anonymous class
            }
        };

        Runnable r2 = () -> {
            System.out.println(this); // refers to outer class
        };
    }
}
```

---

### 5. Functional Interface Requirement

```java id="f6x6"
// Anonymous → works with ANY interface/class

// Lambda → works ONLY with functional interface
```

---

### 6. Variable Scope

```java id="g7x7"
int x = 10;

// Lambda → x must be effectively final
Runnable r = () -> System.out.println(x);
```

```java id="h8x8"
int y = 10;

// Anonymous class → can shadow variables
Runnable r2 = new Runnable() {
    public void run() {
        int y = 20; // allowed
    }
};
```

---

### 7. Performance

```java id="i9x9"
// Anonymous → heavier (class loading)
// Lambda → lighter (JVM optimized)
```

---

### 8. Use Case

```java id="j1y1"
// Anonymous → complex logic, multiple methods

// Lambda → short, single-method logic
```

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Anonymous classes are **verbose class-based implementations**, while Lambdas are **concise function-based implementations using invokedynamic and only for functional interfaces**.

