## Constructor Reference (Java 8)

👉 **Definition (crisp)**
Constructor reference is a way to **refer to a constructor using `ClassName::new` instead of lambda**.

---

## Basic Syntax

```java id="cr1"
ClassName::new
```

---

## Example 1 (No-Arg Constructor)

```java id="cr2"
class Student {
    Student() {
        System.out.println("Created");
    }
}

Supplier<Student> s = Student::new;

Student st = s.get();
```

---

## Equivalent Lambda

```java id="cr3"
Supplier<Student> s = () -> new Student();
```

---

## Example 2 (Parameterized Constructor)

```java id="cr4"
class Student {
    int id;

    Student(int id) {
        this.id = id;
    }
}

Function<Integer, Student> f = Student::new;

Student st = f.apply(101);
```

---

## Example 3 (Multiple Arguments)

```java id="cr5"
class Student {
    int id;
    String name;

    Student(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }
}

BiFunction<Integer, String, Student> f = Student::new;

Student st = f.apply(1, "Prem");
```

---

## Key Points 🔥

* Uses `::new`
* Works with functional interfaces
* Cleaner than lambda
* Constructor signature must match interface method

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Constructor reference is a **shorthand to create objects using `ClassName::new` instead of a lambda expression**.

