## When to Use Method Reference instead of Lambda

---

## 1. When Lambda Just Calls Existing Method ✅

```java id="mr1"
// Lambda
list.forEach(x -> System.out.println(x));

// Method Reference ✅
list.forEach(System.out::println);
```

---

## 2. When Logic is Already Defined (Reuse)

```java id="mr2"
// Lambda
Function<String, Integer> f = s -> Integer.parseInt(s);

// Method Reference ✅
Function<String, Integer> f = Integer::parseInt;
```

---

## 3. For Cleaner & Readable Code

```java id="mr3"
// Lambda
list.sort((a, b) -> a.compareTo(b));

// Method Reference ✅
list.sort(String::compareTo);
```

---

## 4. Constructor Calls

```java id="mr4"
// Lambda
Supplier<List<String>> s = () -> new ArrayList<>();

// Method Reference ✅
Supplier<List<String>> s = ArrayList::new;
```

---

## 5. Simple Pass-through Operations

```java id="mr5"
// Lambda
list.stream().map(x -> x.toUpperCase());

// Method Reference ✅
list.stream().map(String::toUpperCase);
```

---

## When NOT to Use ❌

```java id="mr6"
// Complex logic → use Lambda
x -> {
    int y = x * 2;
    return y + 1;
}
```

---

## Rule of Thumb 🔥

```java id="mr7"
// If Lambda = "just calling a method"
// → replace with method reference
```

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Use method reference when a lambda **only calls an existing method**, making code more concise and readable.

