## Predicate vs Function (Java 8)

---

## 1. Purpose

```java id="pf1"
// Predicate → condition check (true/false)
Predicate<Integer> p = x -> x > 10;

// Function → transformation (input → output)
Function<Integer, String> f = x -> "Val: " + x;
```

---

## 2. Method

```java id="pf2"
// Predicate
boolean test(T t);

// Function
R apply(T t);
```

---

## 3. Return Type

```java id="pf3"
// Predicate → boolean
p.test(5); // true/false

// Function → any type
f.apply(5); // "Val: 5"
```

---

## 4. Usage in Streams

```java id="pf4"
List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(1,2,3,4);

// Predicate → filter
list.stream()
    .filter(x -> x % 2 == 0)
    .forEach(System.out::println);

// Function → map
list.stream()
    .map(x -> x * 2)
    .forEach(System.out::println);
```

---

## 5. Chaining

```java id="pf5"
// Predicate
p1.and(p2).or(p3).negate();

// Function
f1.andThen(f2).compose(f3);
```

---

## Summary 🔥

```java id="pf6"
// Predicate<T>  → T -> boolean (condition)
// Function<T,R> → T -> R (transform)
```

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Predicate is used for **boolean condition checking**, while Function is used for **input-to-output transformation**.

