## Supplier vs Consumer (Java 8)

---

## 1. Purpose

```java id="sc1"
// Supplier → provides data (no input)
Supplier<String> s = () -> "Hello";

// Consumer → consumes data (no return)
Consumer<String> c = str -> System.out.println(str);
```

---

## 2. Method

```java id="sc2"
// Supplier
T get();

// Consumer
void accept(T t);
```

---

## 3. Input / Output

```java id="sc3"
// Supplier → () -> T
String val = s.get();

// Consumer → T -> void
c.accept("Hello");
```

---

## 4. Usage Example

```java id="sc4"
// Supplier → generate value
Supplier<Double> random = () -> Math.random();

// Consumer → use value
Consumer<Double> print = x -> System.out.println(x);

print.accept(random.get());
```

---

## 5. Real Use Case 🔥

```java id="sc5"
// Supplier (lazy value)
Optional<String> opt = Optional.ofNullable(null);

String val = opt.orElseGet(() -> "Default");

// Consumer (side effect)
list.forEach(System.out::println);
```

---

## Summary 🔥

```java id="sc6"
// Supplier<T> → () -> T      (no input, returns value)
// Consumer<T> → T -> void    (takes input, no return)
```

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Supplier **produces data without input**, while Consumer **accepts data and performs an operation without returning anything**.

