## BiFunction (Java 8)

👉 **Definition (crisp)**
`BiFunction<T, U, R>` represents a function that **takes 2 inputs and returns 1 output**.

---

## Method

```java id="bf1"
R apply(T t, U u);
```

---

## Basic Example

```java id="bf2"
BiFunction<Integer, Integer, Integer> add = (a, b) -> a + b;

System.out.println(add.apply(10, 20)); // 30
```

---

## Different Types Example

```java id="bf3"
BiFunction<String, String, Integer> f = (a, b) -> (a + b).length();

System.out.println(f.apply("Hello", "Java")); // length
```

---

## With Object Creation 🔥

```java id="bf4"
class Student {
    int id;
    String name;

    Student(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }
}

BiFunction<Integer, String, Student> f = Student::new;

Student s = f.apply(1, "Prem");
```

---

## Chaining (andThen)

```java id="bf5"
BiFunction<Integer, Integer, Integer> add = (a, b) -> a + b;

Function<Integer, String> after = x -> "Result: " + x;

Function<Integer, String> result = add.andThen(after);

System.out.println(result.apply(2, 3));
```

---

## Summary 🔥

```java id="bf6"
// BiFunction<T,U,R> → (T,U) -> R
```

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 `BiFunction` is a functional interface that **takes two inputs and produces a result**.

