## Collection vs Stream (Java 8)

---

## 1. Basic Idea

```java id="cs1"
// Collection → stores data
List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(1,2,3);

// Stream → processes data
list.stream().forEach(System.out::println);
```

---

## 2. Storage

```java id="cs2"
// Collection → stores elements in memory

// Stream → does NOT store data, only processes
```

---

## 3. Nature

```java id="cs3"
// Collection → data structure

// Stream → computation pipeline
```

---

## 3. Traversal

```java id="cs4"
// Collection → can iterate multiple times
for (int i : list) {}

// Stream → can be consumed only once
Stream<Integer> s = list.stream();
s.forEach(System.out::println);
// s.forEach(...) ❌ error
```

---

## 4. Processing Style

```java id="cs5"
// Collection → external iteration
for (int i : list) {}

// Stream → internal iteration
list.stream().forEach(System.out::println);
```

---

## 5. Lazy vs Eager

```java id="cs6"
// Collection → eager

// Stream → lazy (intermediate ops)
list.stream()
    .filter(x -> x > 2)
    .map(x -> x * 2);
```

---

## 6. Performance

```java id="cs7"
// Collection → sequential

// Stream → supports parallel processing
list.parallelStream().forEach(System.out::println);
```

---

## 7. Modification

```java id="cs8"
// Collection → can modify
list.add(10);

// Stream → cannot modify source
```

---

## Summary 🔥

```java id="cs9"
// Collection → stores data
// Stream → processes data

// Collection → reusable
// Stream → single-use

// Collection → external iteration
// Stream → internal iteration
```

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Collection is used to **store data**, while Stream is used to **process data in a functional and efficient way**.

