## map() vs flatMap() (Java 8)

---

## 1. Basic Idea

```java id="mf1"
// map → 1 element → 1 element
.map(x -> x * 2)

// flatMap → 1 element → multiple elements + flatten
.flatMap(x -> Stream.of(x, x * 2))
```

---

## 2. Return Type

```java id="mf2"
// map → Stream<R>
Stream<String> s1 = list.stream().map(x -> x.toString());

// flatMap → flattened Stream<R>
Stream<String> s2 = list.stream().flatMap(x -> Stream.of(x.toString()));
```

---

## 3. Nested Structure 🔥

```java id="mf3"
List<List<Integer>> list = Arrays.asList(
    Arrays.asList(1,2),
    Arrays.asList(3,4)
);

// map ❌ → Stream<Stream<Integer>>
list.stream()
    .map(l -> l.stream());

// flatMap ✅ → Stream<Integer>
list.stream()
    .flatMap(l -> l.stream())
    .forEach(System.out::println);
```

---

## 4. Real Use Case

```java id="mf4"
// map → simple transformation
List<String> names = users.stream()
    .map(u -> u.name)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

// flatMap → nested list flatten
List<String> roles = users.stream()
    .flatMap(u -> u.roles.stream())
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

---

## 5. Visualization 🔥

```java id="mf5"
// map
[1,2,3] → [2,4,6]

// flatMap
[[1,2],[3,4]] → [1,2,3,4]
```

---

## Summary 🔥

```java id="mf6"
// map → one-to-one transformation
// flatMap → one-to-many + flatten
```

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 `map()` transforms each element individually, while `flatMap()` transforms and **flattens nested structures into a single stream**.

